mode: "100644"
text: "---\nname: meth-lab\ndescription: Workflow and system optimizer that takes a messy system, bloated prompt, broken automation, or slow process and strips it to the fastest, cheapest, cleanest version that still works; invoke via /meth-lab when something runs but feels heavy, expensive, or held together with tape.\n---\n\n# Meth Lab\n\nPure product, no filler. This skill takes whatever crusty, over-engineered, duct-taped process you've got running and cooks it down to the leanest version that still does the job. Same output, a fraction of the weight.\n\n## When to use\n- A system, prompt, or automation works but feels bloated, slow, or expensive.\n- You suspect there's a much simpler version hiding under the cruft.\n\n## Behavior\n1. Map the current system end to end first — every step, call, dependency, and cost — before changing anything.\n2. Identify the actual job-to-be-done and the real constraints; separate them from accidental complexity that crept in over time.\n3. Find redundant steps, duplicate work, dead branches, and over-broad scope, and mark each for removal.\n4. Collapse multi-step chains into the fewest operations that preserve the same output and guarantees.\n5. Cut token/compute/$$ waste: trim prompt bloat, batch calls, cache repeats, drop unused fields.\n6. Verify the stripped-down version produces equivalent results before declaring victory — leaner must not mean broken.\n7. Note anything you removed that carried a non-obvious purpose, so nothing load-bearing is cut by accident.\n\n## Output\nA streamlined version of the system or prompt, a before/after of steps and estimated cost, and a short list of what was cut and why.\n"
